WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.060
- I was riding a city
bike the other day and

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:05.780
someone screamed, "Dr. Mike,
we want more House M.D."

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.183
Here we go, House M.D. Number Three.

00:00:08.183 --> 00:00:09.075
Bee-whoop!

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:11.908
(soft beat music)

00:00:16.477 --> 00:00:18.392
- [Lady] Oh, God.

00:00:18.392 --> 00:00:21.648
- [Boy] Is the baby coming?
I don't know how to do this.

00:00:21.648 --> 00:00:22.751
- [Dr. Mike] Aww.

00:00:22.751 --> 00:00:24.062
- You okay, Ian?

00:00:24.062 --> 00:00:25.145
- Yeah, sure.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.353
- Uh-oh.

00:00:28.353 --> 00:00:29.197
- [Lady] I don't think you are.

00:00:29.197 --> 00:00:32.473
- Are they both going to be
patients in this episode?

00:00:32.473 --> 00:00:33.573
- You're bleeding.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:38.193
- Wow, that's a lot of blood.

00:00:41.958 --> 00:00:43.093
Hmm, a little poker.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.390
- Dr. Cuddy, I've got one
of your patients in the E.R.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:47.955
Ian Austin, six years old.

00:00:47.955 --> 00:00:49.270
- Oh, I know him.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:50.103
What's the problem?

00:00:50.103 --> 00:00:51.130
I'm all in.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.030
- Bloody diarrhea, hemodynamically stable,

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:55.160
but he's been developing
some coordination problems.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.180
- Sounds like gastroenteritis
and dehydration.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.157
Order fluids and I'll
take it on my service.

00:00:59.157 --> 00:01:00.732
Bets to you House.

00:01:00.732 --> 00:01:01.565
- Did they scan his head?

00:01:01.565 --> 00:01:02.640
- No, why would they scan his -

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.473
- Don't play games.

00:01:03.473 --> 00:01:04.470
You gonna call?

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:05.830
- How's the heart rate?

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:06.670
- Stable.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:07.710
- [Cuddy] I'm sorry.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.930
House, it's gastroenteritis.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.163
I'm not going anywhere.

00:01:11.163 --> 00:01:13.760
Put the order in and
someone tell Allen and Sarah

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.260
that I'll be up when I'm done.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.020
- I hope that's an nonalcoholic
beverage in her hand.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.530
If you're on-call and you're going to

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.220
be admitting patients to your service,

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.820
you probably shouldn't be
drinking alcohol, just saying.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.122
- [Cuddy] Stone cold bluff!

00:01:26.122 --> 00:01:29.100
You might wanna spend a
little more time paying

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.470
attention to your cards
and a little less time

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.020
starring at my breasts.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.190
- They don't match either.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.980
- I used to play poker semi-professionally

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:38.290
during my college days.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.305
I would play it online
before it was banned

00:01:40.305 --> 00:01:44.210
and I would make some
money, not a lot of money.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:45.760
It was more about a learning experience,

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.570
but the friends that I played with,

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.830
we constantly used poker as an analogy

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.380
for everything we did in life,

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:54.790
whether it was medicine, talking to girls,

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:57.040
relationships with our friends,

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.120
we would always somehow
relate it back to poker.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.326
And I feel like that's what's
gonna happen in this episode.

00:02:01.326 --> 00:02:03.440
Everything that's gonna
happen with this patient

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.140
is somehow gonna turn into a poker hand.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:06.840
What's interesting about poker

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.840
is that it's a lot like life.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.740
Making a difficult
decision, you're not always

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.912
gonna have all your information available

00:02:12.912 --> 00:02:14.760
at your disposal, right?

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.020
Because life is crazy and
things change all the time,

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:18.110
there's variables.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:20.100
So there's a lot of luck involved in life.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.900
That's the same thing
that happens in poker.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.670
Skill can only take you so far.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.210
There's a huge luck component.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.280
If your opponent only has
one card that will beat you,

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.270
despite the fact that every other card

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:31.520
is gonna work in your favor, they may win.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.830
And if they win, it doesn't
mean that they out played you.

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:35.080
It means that they got lucky.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.630
The same thing that can happen in life.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.010
You can make a great decision with

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.022
all the information
available at your disposal,

00:02:40.022 --> 00:02:42.970
and yet you can still lose
or have a bad outcome.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:44.670
- Follow my finger with your eyes.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:49.763
- Nystagmus.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.565
- How much longer will Dr. Cuddy be?

00:02:53.565 --> 00:02:55.620
- Well given the number
of mojitos she's knocking

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.420
back at a party, I'd say
it's gonna be a least

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.420
three hours before she's even conscious.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.630
- [Mother] Weren't you at the same party?

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:03.463
- I don't drink.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.430
- I just pop pills!

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:08.343
- I want you to reach
out and grab my cane.

00:03:09.697 --> 00:03:12.697
(suspenseful music)

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:15.503
- What's wrong?

00:03:15.503 --> 00:03:17.010
- Abnormal proprioception.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.940
- Your son's brain is losing
control of his muscles.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.370
- Dr. Cuddy's message said it was

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.630
just dehydration from diarrhea.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:23.463
- She's wrong.

00:03:23.463 --> 00:03:24.890
- I love House, but the way that he jumps

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:27.350
to knowing that there's
something more sinister

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:30.483
happening with this child
from hearing the basic story,

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:32.330
it's a little extra.

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:34.030
Some children, when they have diarrhea,

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:36.290
especially bloody diarrhea,
with stable vital signs,

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:38.770
stable labs, can act a little bit strange

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.330
and then as time goes on, they improve.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.610
By him taking a special
interest in this story,

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.350
just from hearing that story,
it's a little unrealistic.

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.970
- Two cases, same symptoms.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:52.480
What do six year olds and
70 year olds have in common?

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.350
- [Foreman] This kid
doesn't have kidney failure.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:55.183
- [House] He will.

00:03:55.183 --> 00:03:57.180
I was treating a 73 year old woman

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.270
who went through this
progression of symptoms,

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:00.847
the last of which was -

00:04:04.373 --> 00:04:07.120
And in case any of you missed
that class in med school,

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:08.070
that one's untreatable.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.180
Do a colonoscopy.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:10.170
- On a six year old kid who probably has

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:11.930
nothing worse than some food poisoning?

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.270
- If you happen to find
any purple papules,

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:15.730
do me a favor and grab a slice.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:17.640
I wanna check for Erdheim-Chester.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:18.760
- How does he know what that is?

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:19.750
I've never even heard of it.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:22.240
I don't know how you
justify doing a colonoscopy

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.990
on a child with a single
episode of bloody diarrhea

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:27.714
and a stomach ache, with normal
vital signs, normal labs,

00:04:27.714 --> 00:04:30.849
just off of the fear that
there could be a rare,

00:04:30.849 --> 00:04:32.915
potentially life-threatening condition

00:04:32.915 --> 00:04:35.970
that hasn't yet presented
itself in any other way

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.770
outside of ataxia and bloody diarrhea.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.550
Doing a colonoscopy, while
it's a common procedure,

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.651
it's a benign procedure,
things can go wrong.

00:04:42.651 --> 00:04:45.675
You can rupture the colon,
you can have an infection.

00:04:45.675 --> 00:04:48.210
I mean if something
negative happens as a result

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.930
of the colonoscopy and you find nothing,

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:51.080
that's horrible!

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:52.410
You did that to the child.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:54.540
- You know how people see the Virgin Mary

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.053
in danishes and stuff?

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:00.250
Someone died 12 years ago
and House doesn't know why.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:05.160
House sees that case now in
paint peeling and clouds.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:05.993
- I agree with him.

00:05:05.993 --> 00:05:08.310
And yes, like I said before,

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:10.710
residents don't do all the procedures.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.306
You would get a gastroenterologist
to do the colonoscopy.

00:05:13.306 --> 00:05:15.140
Internal medicine residents don't

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:17.810
just do colonoscopies on their own.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:18.750
This is crazy.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.160
Why am I getting so upset about this?

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.067
It's just a show.

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:23.890
- Negative for Erdheim-Chester.

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:24.980
- Let me see.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:27.104
- Still don't know what that is.

00:05:27.104 --> 00:05:30.104
(suspenseful music)

00:05:33.426 --> 00:05:34.660
- It's not Erdheim-Chester.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:36.110
- It's exactly what we said before,

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.450
a gun variety gastroenteritis.

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:39.800
Can we go back to the party?

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:41.730
- Do a kidney biopsy.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:42.563
- What?

00:05:42.563 --> 00:05:44.900
Subjecting a child to this many procedures

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.370
that are not benign on a whim is crazy.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.210
There's no proof that
this is what's happening.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:50.470
You have to monitor the child,

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:52.633
watch the symptoms, act clinically.

00:05:55.009 --> 00:05:55.842
Ego, check it.

00:05:55.842 --> 00:05:56.980
- [Dr. Chase] The colonoscopy was clean

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:59.350
and the biopsy was negative
for Erdheim-Chester.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:00.410
- So he's gonna be all right,

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:01.960
it was just some sort of virus.

00:06:03.093 --> 00:06:04.260
- What's that?

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:06.720
- Urine.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:07.773
- But it's brown.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.330
- His kidneys are shutting down.

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:13.210
- Still think it's not the same case?

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:15.400
- Now you can start thinking
that it's something else.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.930
- Doctor had a patient a while back who

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:19.780
exhibits the same symptoms as your son.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:21.393
- Then, you know what's wrong.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:23.103
- No.

00:06:23.103 --> 00:06:24.560
- So, what do you know?

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.142
- We know the likely course
the disease will take.

00:06:27.142 --> 00:06:28.540
- [Father] Which is?

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:30.480
- She had multiple system failures -

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:31.630
- What happened to her?

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:38.900
- She died 24 hours after her admission.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:41.460
- Look, telling patients
information once you know it,

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:43.870
is really important
because you want to honor

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:46.670
the idea of autonomy where
a patient has the ability

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:49.590
to act upon their own body with the

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:50.880
best information possible.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.870
In this case, it'd be the patients parents

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:54.820
because the child can't
make decisions for himself.

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:57.180
However, if you have
incomplete information

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.080
and that information is very worrisome

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.390
and is not gonna bring
any value to the patient

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:02.960
and only gonna bring anxiety,

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.050
maybe best leave that information out

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.350
until you have a more
concrete plan or answer.

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:10.040
Otherwise, we're just
creating a lot of anxiety,

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:11.780
a lot of worry and essentially telling

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:13.063
these parents your child might die today

00:07:13.063 --> 00:07:15.530
because we don't know what we're doing.

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:16.560
- [Cameron] The base of his brain has been

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.050
infiltrated by a small mass.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:18.883
We think -

00:07:18.883 --> 00:07:19.716
- Pituitary?

00:07:19.716 --> 00:07:20.549
- Looks that way.

00:07:20.549 --> 00:07:21.980
- Explains the low blood pressure.

00:07:24.309 --> 00:07:26.533
- Pretty much confirms the lymphoma.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:34.060
If we'd started Ester on prednisone -

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.540
- Uh, did anyone see the lymphoma?

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:38.070
- [Cameron] No, we saw a mass.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:39.460
The location was consistent with -

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:41.410
- Didn't see any in the blood either.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:43.090
White blood cells show no spindling

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:46.260
or abnormal nuclei, nothing
on immunochemistries either.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:47.313
It's not lymphoma.

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.618
- Can't breathe.

00:07:51.618 --> 00:07:52.829
- Uh-oh.

00:07:52.829 --> 00:07:53.934
- Chase!

00:07:53.934 --> 00:07:55.434
*monitors beeping*

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:57.435
- [Mother] What's happening?

00:07:57.435 --> 00:07:59.244
- Sounds like he's having an anaphylactic

00:07:59.244 --> 00:08:01.694
reaction to something and
his throats closing up.

00:08:02.817 --> 00:08:04.457
- [Mother] Ian, come on.

00:08:04.457 --> 00:08:05.957
Honey, just relax.

00:08:07.092 --> 00:08:08.092
Ian breathe.

00:08:10.304 --> 00:08:11.889
- [Chase] Look at the right atrium.

00:08:11.889 --> 00:08:13.265
- That's not kawasakis.

00:08:13.265 --> 00:08:14.670
- No.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.840
It's small, but it's there.

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:19.170
- [Foreman] Ester didn't
have a mass in her heart.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:20.080
- Ian's younger.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:21.680
He can take more of a pounding.

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:23.730
Ester died before the
disease reached her heart.

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:25.890
The disease made a mass and made it fast.

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:27.000
- Could be bacteria.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:27.833
- Muscle.

00:08:27.833 --> 00:08:28.666
- Connective tissue.

00:08:28.666 --> 00:08:30.670
- [House] The kid can't
take anymore theories.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:32.570
Only thing we know is that
whatever that mass is,

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:33.769
that's what he's got.

00:08:33.769 --> 00:08:35.363
We need a piece of it.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:37.780
I'm doing a biopsy.

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:39.660
- You're gonna do a
biopsy on the kid's heart!

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:41.180
Come on, House!

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:42.063
- And out again.

00:08:43.674 --> 00:08:45.800
*monitors beeping*

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:47.082
- Cardiac arrest.

00:08:47.082 --> 00:08:48.249
Code blue.

00:08:48.249 --> 00:08:49.310
- Code blue.

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:50.260
- Come on, paddles!

00:08:51.453 --> 00:08:54.080
Chest compressions, chest compressions,

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.333
chest compressions, before you shock!

00:08:56.333 --> 00:08:57.288
*shock*

00:08:57.288 --> 00:08:58.141
- [House] Again.

00:08:58.141 --> 00:08:59.270
*shock*

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:01.300
- So when you shock, you
right away go back to

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:04.250
chest compressions, chest
compressions, chest compressions.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:08.690
All this time without chest compressions

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:10.820
is the blood not circulating.

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:12.584
Can someone do some chest compressions?

00:09:12.584 --> 00:09:14.834
- Wait, I've got something.

00:09:16.446 --> 00:09:17.540
- He's back.

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:20.240
- [Wilson] He can't
stand it, he goes all in.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:21.743
He's sure he's won.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:26.243
I call, I flip 'em.

00:09:26.243 --> 00:09:27.076
Oo!

00:09:27.076 --> 00:09:28.693
- Pocket aces.

00:09:28.693 --> 00:09:31.163
- I nailed his ass.

00:09:32.169 --> 00:09:35.169
(suspenseful music)

00:09:36.942 --> 00:09:39.640
- What did he get from that story?

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:40.610
God, you gotta love this.

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:41.760
- The aces were hiding all along.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:44.350
- Oh, the aces were hiding, duh.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:46.297
- Test him for Erdheim-Chester disease.

00:09:46.297 --> 00:09:47.518
*Dr. Mike laughs*

00:09:47.518 --> 00:09:49.940
- All right, I'm pausing it.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:52.100
I'm looking up what Erdheim-Chester is

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:53.456
because this is ridiculous.

00:09:53.456 --> 00:09:56.100
Erdheim-Chester Disease.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:59.267
It's a rare type of slow
growing blood cancer.

00:09:59.267 --> 00:10:02.243
In Erdheim-Chester the excess
production of histiocytes,

00:10:02.243 --> 00:10:05.880
AKA histiocytosis, leads
to inflammation that can

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.870
damage organs and tissues
throughout the body

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:10.216
causing them to be thick
and dense, scared fibrotic,

00:10:10.216 --> 00:10:12.936
and this tissue damage
may lead to organ failure.

00:10:12.936 --> 00:10:15.580
I thought they tested
for this and if they did

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:17.750
a kidney biopsy, they
would have found this.

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:19.230
I don't know where he's going with this.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:21.070
This is a disease that probably happens

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:24.210
one in a million, kind of
appropriate for a House episode.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:25.200
- He tested negative.

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:26.380
- So did Ester.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:27.540
- The disease lied.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.210
- [Cameron] Yeah, the
tumors got it in for you.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:30.626
Diseases don't lie.

00:10:30.626 --> 00:10:31.620
- Fine, it didn't lie.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:33.020
It slow played us.

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:36.850
We biopsied the colon, it
hadn't reached the GI tract yet.

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:38.405
It's there now.

00:10:38.405 --> 00:10:39.760
It's in his liver, his lungs.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.610
- You want it to be there,

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:44.240
because then you didn't
screw up 12 years ago.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:46.020
- We can't waste our one test on the

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:47.478
one disease we know it's not.

00:10:47.478 --> 00:10:50.751
(suspenseful music)

00:10:50.751 --> 00:10:51.584
- Run the test.

00:10:51.584 --> 00:10:53.335
- Why didn't they do the kidney biopsy?

00:10:53.335 --> 00:10:56.335
(suspenseful music)

00:10:57.260 --> 00:10:59.713
- Take your time and say it loud.

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:05.063
- They turn red.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.613
- CD68 positive.

00:11:11.613 --> 00:11:13.030
*House hits wall*

00:11:14.372 --> 00:11:17.412
*Dr. Mike laughs*

00:11:17.412 --> 00:11:18.623
- It's so dramatic.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:22.687
I feel like -

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:24.740
- Start the treatment.

00:11:28.997 --> 00:11:31.960
- This is how the US National Library

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:32.850
of Medicine describes it.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:35.670
It's a rare type of slow
growing blood cancer.

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:37.840
How is this child dying in 48 hours?

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:39.150
I don't know.

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:40.790
Something about this seems shady.

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:42.807
Had they'd just followed regular protocol,

00:11:42.807 --> 00:11:45.250
consulted the proper physicians,

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:48.830
instead of having House
run a muck with this case,

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:51.380
this would have been
solved so much quicker,

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:53.980
so much simpler, without
the child suffering

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:55.400
these side effects.

00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:57.860
- So Ester can rest peaceful now, huh?

00:11:58.850 --> 00:11:59.683
- Yeah.

00:11:59.683 --> 00:12:01.360
- House M.D.'s awesome, but check out

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:03.140
these hilarious two videos.

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:04.400
I know you're gonna love them.

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:06.308
And stay happy and healthy.

00:12:06.308 --> 00:12:09.141
(soft beat music)

